export const INSTRUCTIONS = "Video farm: turns the user's audio + caption file into a finished MP4 promo or explainer video. You design the scenes, the farm renders them on GitHub Actions (4 cores) with sound effects, then you mix the user's audio in.\n\nThere are two looks (\"brand themes\"): handson (the Hands-on AI website) and channel (the physics channel, which also has layouts for formulas, graphs, forces and simulations; call get_guide(\"channel-physics\")).\nWhen the user sends audio and captions and did not say which look to use, ask exactly ONE short question first: \"Hands-on AI theme or your channel theme?\" Ask nothing else. If they already said (hands-on, website, channel, physics), do not ask. Then say in one line what you chose and do the work. Defaults: 16:9, theme light for handson and dark for channel, captions bar on, top bar on, mascot off, sound effects on (minimal pack, volume 0.9), audience everyone (plain words on screen; use expert only if the viewers study physics). Apply anything the user states (reel/shorts = 9:16, dark, brand name, CTA, URL).\nAlways set the choice in both places: \"meta\": {\"brandTheme\": \"channel\"} inside scenes, and the option brand_theme when you call submit_render. Layouts of one brand are not available in the other (validate_scenes tells you).\n\nFlow\n1. First time in a chat: call get_guide(\"scene-guide\") for the scene format and layouts (and get_guide(\"channel-physics\") for the channel brand). Other topics: call get_guide with no topic to list them (design, icons-and-logos, sound-effects and more). The topics \"workflow\" and \"remote-build\" describe the older local skill procedure; follow the flow below instead.\n2. Read the captions and design scenes.json yourself: hook first (title, with **key words** highlighted), then the layout that shows each idea (logos for named tools, facts or stat for numbers, features for benefits, bullets or steps for lists, table for old vs new, code for commands), last scene outro with a CTA. Scene start/end are copied exactly from caption times, no gaps or overlaps. On-screen text is shorter than the spoken words. Never the same layout three times in a row.\n3. Call validate_scenes (captions + scenes). Fix every error before rendering.\n4. Call submit_render (captions text, scenes JSON text, options). Poll render_status every 20 to 30 s until success.\n5. Call get_video. It returns the video URL and 4 preview frame URLs. Download them in the sandbox with curl -L. LOOK at the preview frames (text cut off, overlap with the top bar or caption bar, empty scenes). If something is wrong, fix scenes.json and render again. Do not say it is fine without looking.\n6. Mix the user's audio into the render (the render has sound effects only, no narration):\n   ffmpeg -y -i remote.mp4 -i USER_AUDIO -filter_complex \"[1:a]volume=1[v];[0:a][v]amix=inputs=2:duration=first:normalize=0[a]\" -map 0:v -map \"[a]\" -c:v copy -c:a aac -b:a 192k -shortest final.mp4\n7. Send final.mp4 to the user with one or two lines about the scenes, and offer to change one scene.\n\nRules\n- Never add background music or any ambient bed, in any video, including demos and tests. The only audio is the user's audio plus the sound effects.\n- The user's audio never goes through a tool call; it stays in the sandbox and is mixed in locally.\n- The repo is public: tell the user once that captions and scenes text is visible in the Actions run and the video is a public link for 24 hours. Do not use it for private content.\n- Do not invent images. Logos come from brand names (simpleicons slugs), icons from Lucide names.\n- If the user has no audio, say so; do not make up narration. If a track is needed for a test, use silence.\n";
export const GUIDES = {
 "workflow": "# Fully automatic: the user sends only audio and captions\nNever add background music. There are two looks (\"brand themes\"): `handson` (the Hands-on AI website) and `channel` (the physics channel, with extra layouts for formulas, graphs, forces and simulations; read `references/channel-physics.md`). **If the user did not say which one, ask exactly one short question before anything else: \"Hands-on AI theme or your channel theme?\"** Do not ask anything else. If they already said (hands-on, website, channel, physics) do not ask. Then use these defaults and say in one line what you chose: 16:9, theme light for `handson` and dark for `channel`, captions bar on, mascot off, top bar on, sound effects on (`minimal`), `brandTheme` in `meta`, audience `everyone` (plain words; `expert` only if the viewers study physics). Ask nothing else unless the message gives no audio or no captions. If they mention a shape (reel, shorts, vertical), a theme, a brand name, a CTA or a URL, apply it.\n\nYou are the scene designer. The script does the plumbing, you do the thinking.\n\n1. **Read the captions** (`.srt`, `.vtt` or JSON) and understand the story: hook, problem, solution, proof, call to action.\n2. **Write `scenes.json` yourself** (read `references/scene-guide.md` first). For each beat pick the layout that shows the idea, not just repeats the words:\n   - tools, partners or tech named in the narration -> `logos` (slugs from simpleicons.org)\n   - a number or percentage -> `stat` or `facts`\n   - two to four benefits -> `features` with a fitting Lucide icon and a different `anim` on each card\n   - a list or sequence -> `bullets` with icons, or `steps`\n   - old way versus new way -> `table`\n   - a code or command -> `code`\n   - hook first -> `title` (with `**key words**` highlighted), close -> `outro` with a CTA\n   Scene `start`/`end` are copied from caption times, no gaps or overlaps. On-screen text is shorter than the spoken words. Vary layouts; never the same one three times in a row. Wrap 1 to 2 key words per scene in `**`.\n3. **Build with one command:**\n   `python scripts/build_video.py --audio <audio> --captions <captions> --scenes scenes.json --out out/video.mp4 [--aspect 9:16] [--brand-theme handson|channel] [--theme light|dark] [--brand \"Name\"] [--no-topbar] [--no-captions] [--sfx minimal|off] [--low]`\n   It sets up the project once (reused after that, about 30 s on the first run only), parses the captions, validates, renders, and saves 4 preview frames. Run it in the background (`nohup ... &`) and poll the log; a 30 s 1080p video takes a few minutes.\n   Fallback: without `--scenes` it makes a rough set of scenes from the captions by keywords. Use that only for a quick draft; your own scenes are better.\n   All options can also go in a `--job job.json` file (same names, underscores).\n4. **Look before you send.** View the 4 PNGs in `out/preview/` (and extra frames with ffmpeg for the logos, table and outro scenes). Check text cut off, overlap with the top bar or caption bar, boxes instead of glyphs, empty scenes. Fix `scenes.json` and rebuild. Do not say it is fine without looking.\n5. **Deliver** the MP4 with `SendUserFile`. Say in one or two lines what the scenes are and offer to change one scene.\n\nTo change one scene later: edit only that scene in `scenes.json` and run the same command again.\n\nIf the sandbox has no text-to-speech engine and the user has no audio, say so; do not invent narration.\n",
 "remote-build": "# Fast remote build on GitHub (optional)\nLocal rendering here has only 2 CPU cores, so a 30 s video takes 2 to 3 minutes. A **public** GitHub repo running the `video-farm` workflows renders on 4 cores (a private repo gets 2, no gain). Two ways to use it, in this order:\n\n**A. MCP tools (when the `video-farm` connector is available, tools `submit_render`, `render_status`, `get_video`, `cancel_render`, `list_jobs`)**\n1. Write `scenes.json` as usual. Call `submit_render` with `captions` (the caption file text), `scenes` (the scenes.json text) and `options` (aspect, theme, brand, cta, url, sfx, sfx_volume, no_topbar...). Keep the payload under 60,000 characters. You get a `job_id`.\n2. Poll `render_status` every 20 to 30 s until `success` (or `failed`: open the run link and fix the cause). Typical: 1 to 2 minutes for 30 s of video.\n3. `get_video` returns a public URL. Download it: `curl -L -o remote.mp4 \"<url>\"` (github.com hosts are reachable from the sandbox).\n4. **The remote render has the sound effects but no narration** (audio is too large to pass through a tool call). Mix the user's audio in: `python scripts/mux_audio.py remote.mp4 <user audio> out/video.mp4`. Never add music.\n5. Deliver `out/video.mp4`. The remote render produces no preview frames; if you changed a layout, check it with a quick local `--low` build of that scene.\nPrivacy: only captions and scenes text reach the public repo (visible in the Actions run); the audio never leaves the sandbox. Tell the user once. Releases are deleted after 24 h.\n\n**B. Git (no MCP):** attach the repo with `add_repo` (access `push`), clone it, then `python scripts/remote_build.py --repo-dir <clone> --audio A --captions C --scenes scenes.json --out out/video.mp4`. This one uploads the audio to the public repo (public and in git history); warn the user. Do not use `gh`, its token is not available.\n\nIf neither is set up, build locally and mention the option once. Setup is in the `video-farm` package README.\n",
 "design": "# Design: keep it the same as the website\nAll design values live in `assets/template/src/theme.ts` and are explained in `references/design-system.md`. Do not hard-code colours or sizes in scenes. If the user wants a new look, change `theme.ts` once. If a layout is missing, add a component in `src/scenes/index.tsx` and register it in `registry`, add it to `lib/types.ts` and to `REQ` in `scripts/check_scenes.py`.\n",
 "site-look": "# Site look (updated from the latest website)\n- **Top bar** on every scene: pink \"AI\" square plus the site name, white card with outline and hard shadow. Change the name in `theme.ts` (`site`) or per video with `meta.brand: { \"name\": \"...\", \"mark\": \"AI\" }`. Hide it with `meta.topbar: false`.\n- **Highlighter marks:** wrap words in `**double stars**` in any title, subtitle, bullet or CTA and a mint highlighter sweeps behind them, like `.mark` on the site. Use 1 to 2 marks per scene, on the key words only.\n- **Colour bands:** each scene is painted like a site section. `bullets`, `features` and `steps` use the white `surface` band (cards turn cream with a soft shadow and no outline); `outro` uses the blue band with a white CTA card and 6px shadow; everything else uses the cream page. Override per scene with `\"band\": \"bg\" | \"surface\" | \"blue\" | \"pink\" | \"lav\" | \"mint\" | \"sun\"`.\n- **`facts` layout:** up to 3 pastel cards with a big number and one line, like the site's problem facts (`{ \"value\": \"~4%\", \"text\": \"...\" }`).\n- **`table` layout:** the site's compare table, blue header, grey old-way column, green ticked column (`head` of 3 labels, up to 5 `rows` of 3 cells).\n- Keep the first column of `head` empty (`\"\"`) when the rows already name the row.\n",
 "icons-and-logos": "# Brand logos and animated icons\nTwo layouts and two options use offline npm packages already listed in `package.json` (`simple-icons`, `lucide-react`). No downloads at render time.\n- `logos` layout: `{ \"layout\": \"logos\", \"title\": \"Built with\", \"items\": [{ \"brand\": \"python\" }, { \"brand\": \"github\", \"label\": \"GitHub\" }] }`. Max 6. `brand` is a slug from simpleicons.org (python, react, github, docker, figma, typescript, vercel, nodedotjs...). Logos sit on white tiles so they read in both themes. `\"mono\": true` draws them all in one dark colour. Some brands (for example OpenAI) are not in the package; an unknown slug stops the render with a clear error, so use a Lucide icon or a user-supplied file in `public/` instead.\n- `features` layout: `{ \"layout\": \"features\", \"items\": [{ \"icon\": \"rocket\", \"title\": \"Learn fast\", \"text\": \"optional line\", \"anim\": \"bounce\" }] }`. Max 4.\n- `bullets` items may be `{ \"text\": \"...\", \"icon\": \"search\", \"anim\": \"pulse\" }` instead of a plain string.\n- `title` scene accepts `\"icon\": \"sparkles\", \"anim\": \"wiggle\"` to show an animated icon tile instead of the mascot.\n- `icon` is any Lucide name (lucide.dev/icons), kebab-case or PascalCase. `anim` is one of `pop` (default), `bounce`, `spin`, `pulse`, `float`, `wiggle`, `draw`, `none`.\n- Use `<BrandLogo name=\"github\" />` and `<AnimIcon name=\"rocket\" anim=\"bounce\" />` / `<IconTile />` inside any new scene component.\n- Other libraries that install fine from npm if the user wants them: `@phosphor-icons/react`, `@tabler/icons-react`, `react-icons`, `@iconify/json`, `@remotion/lottie` + `lottie-web` (needs a Lottie .json file the user provides). Photos and illustrations cannot be downloaded in the sandbox; ask the user to attach them and put them in `public/`.\n- If the sandbox has no text-to-speech engine, tell the user and ask for their own audio. Do not pretend a silent demo has narration.\n",
 "sound-effects": "# Sound effects\nOffline sound effects come from the npm package `uisfx` (MIT, 12 styles x 78 cues, all bundled, no downloads). `build_video.py` turns them on by default with the `minimal` pack at a clear, loud level (volume 0.9, the user asked for them loud). They are the only added audio; there is no background music.\n- `--sfx soft|glass|dreamy|organic|rubber|arcade|scifi|cinematic|mechanical|studio|zen` picks the style; `--sfx off` removes sound; `--sfx-volume 0.5` makes them quieter, `1` is maximum. Pick one style per video. `minimal` or `soft` for most, `arcade` or `rubber` for playful or kids content, `cinematic` for trailers, `zen` for calm.\n- Where sounds play: scene change `swipe` (first scene `open`), cards and bullets landing `drop` (pitch varies per item), logos `select`, table rows `snap`, highlighter `focus`, stat number and CTA button `success`, steps `progress-step`.\n- In your own scene component: `<Pop sfx=\"drop\" pitch={pitch(i)}>` or `<Sfx cue=\"success\" delay={frames} />` (from `lib/sfx.tsx`). To use another cue, add its name to `SFX_CUES` in `lib/sfx.tsx` and in `scripts/build_video.py`.\n- `@remotion/sfx` does not work in the sandbox (it downloads its sounds from a blocked address); do not use it.\n",
 "word-highlight": "# Word-by-word highlight (optional)\nIf the captions JSON has `words: [{text,start,end}]`, the caption bar highlights the spoken word in mint. SRT/VTT have no word times, so the bar shows the whole line. If the user wants highlight and has none, suggest making word timestamps with Whisper (`--word_timestamps True`) and giving the JSON.\n",
 "tamil-and-other-languages": "# Tamil and other languages\nNoto Sans Tamil is bundled and used automatically for Tamil letters, so Tamil text works in titles, bullets and captions with no setup. Keep each on-screen line short; Tamil words are long. For any other script, add a woff2 file to `public/fonts/` and one line to `lib/fonts.ts`.\n",
 "scene-guide": "# Writing scenes.json\n\n```json\n{\n  \"meta\": { \"title\": \"...\", \"duration\": 24, \"fps\": 30, \"aspect\": \"16:9\", \"theme\": \"dark\",\n            \"captions\": \"bar\", \"mascot\": true, \"audio\": \"audio.mp3\" },\n  \"scenes\": [ { \"id\": \"s1\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 3.5, \"layout\": \"title\", \"...\": \"...\" } ]\n}\n```\n`aspect`: 16:9 (1920x1080), 9:16 (1080x1920), 1:1 (1080x1080). `theme`: dark or light. `captions`: bar or off. Optional `accent` on any scene: pink, blue, lav, mint, sun, accent.\n\n## Layouts\n| layout | fields | best for | limits |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| title | eyebrow?, title, subtitle? | opening, section start | title under 50 characters |\n| bullets | eyebrow?, title?, items[] | lists of 2 to 5 points | max 5 items, each under 45 characters |\n| stat | eyebrow?, value, label, note? | one number or short proof (\"14\", \"3x\") | value up to 6 characters |\n| quote | text, who? | a claim, a testimonial, a key sentence | text under 90 characters |\n| code | title?, code | a command or snippet | max 8 lines, 40 characters wide |\n| steps | title?, steps[] | how it works, 2 to 6 steps, highlights one by one | each under 45 characters |\n| compare | left{title,items[]}, right{title,items[]} | before/after, old/new | 3 items per side |\n| outro | title, cta, url?, tagline? | last scene, call to action | cta under 25 characters |\n\n## Picking layouts from captions\n- First scene: `title` (name of the product and a one-line promise).\n- A caption listing things (\"a loop, tools, memory and safety\") becomes `bullets`.\n- A caption with a number becomes `stat`.\n- A sentence that sounds like a claim or a customer voice becomes `quote`.\n- \"First... then... finally\" becomes `steps`.\n- \"Instead of X, Y\" becomes `compare`.\n- A command, file name or snippet becomes `code`.\n- Last scene: `outro`.\n\n## Timing\n- Group 1 to 3 captions per scene. Scenes under 2 seconds feel jumpy; over 8 seconds feel static.\n- Items in `bullets` and `steps` appear spread over the first 70% of the scene, so give them at least 1 second each.\n- Copy times exactly from `captions.json`. `check_scenes.py` rejects any scene that does not start and end on a caption boundary.\n\n## Changing one scene later\nEdit only that object in `scenes.json`, run `check_scenes.py`, then re-render.\n\n\n## logos and features (added)\n- `logos`: 2 to 6 brand names, shown as white tiles. Use when the narration lists tools, partners or tech stack. 4 or more logos make two rows, so keep the title short.\n- `features`: 2 to 4 icon cards for benefits or steps. Pick a different `anim` per card (bounce, wiggle, float) so they do not move in sync.\n- Bullets with icons: give every item an icon or none, do not mix.\n",
 "design-system": "# Design system (copied from the website)\n\nSource: `apps/web/src/styles/globals.css`. The video keeps the same look: cream or dark page, **thick dark outline**, **hard offset shadow with no blur**, rounded corners, pastel blocks, bright green accent.\n\n## Colours\n| Token | Light | Dark |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| bg | `#fff7f0` | `#151823` |\n| surface (cards) | `#ffffff` | `#212638` |\n| ink (text) | `#1e293b` | `#f5efe6` |\n| muted | `#475569` | `#a9b2c6` |\n| line (outline) | `#1e293b` | `#f5efe6` |\n| hair (dividers) | `#eadfd3` | `#323a52` |\n| code bg | `#ececf9` | `#2a3047` |\n\nPastels (same in both themes, text on them is always `#1e293b`): pink `#fbb9ae`, blue `#a8d8ea`, lav `#e4e4f7`, mint `#86efac`, sun `#facc15`. Brand green accent `#22c55e`.\n\n## Shape\nBorder 2.5px, radius 18px, shadow `5px 5px 0` (small: `3px 3px 0`). Pills and badges are fully round with a 2px outline. Dashed outline = locked or upcoming.\n\n## Type\nNunito (400/600/700/800) for everything. Headings are Nunito 700 with letter-spacing -0.02em. The CSS names Fredoka for headings, but the site never loads it, so real pages show Nunito, and the video matches that. Code uses JetBrains Mono. Tamil falls back to Noto Sans Tamil.\n\nVideo type scale on a 1280 canvas (scaled up for 1920 and portrait): h1 76, h2 48, h3 34, body 30, small 24, eyebrow 22, caption 32, code 26.\n\n## Components mapped from the site\n| Website | Video component |\n| --- | --- |\n| `.card` | `Card` |\n| `.eyebrow` | `Eyebrow` (pill above a heading) |\n| `.num` | `Num` (square badge) |\n| `.primary` button | `Button` |\n| `.lessons` list (done / current / locked) | `steps` scene |\n| `.copybox` / code | `code` scene |\n| Robot from the thumbnail | `Mascot` |\n\n## Changing the look\nEdit `src/theme.ts` only. Scenes read everything through `useTheme()`.\n\n\n## Updated from the latest website (globals.css)\n| Website | Video |\n| --- | --- |\n| `.topbar` + `.brand` + `.logo` (pink \"AI\" square, SITE_NAME) | `TopBar` in `Frame` |\n| `.mark` highlighter sweep, `--sel: #86efac` | `Mark` and `Rich` (`**word**`) |\n| `.problems`, `section.how` white full-width bands | `band: \"surface\"`, cards go cream with `--shadow-soft`, no outline |\n| `.cta-band` blue band + `.cta` card, shadow `6px 6px 0` | `outro` layout |\n| `.facts` pastel cards with big display number | `facts` layout |\n| `.compare` table, blue head, green ticked \"us\" column | `table` layout |\n| `.ico` rounded pastel tile, 2px outline, radius 12 | `IconTile` (Lucide icons instead of emoji) |\n| `.primary` button, green `#22c55e`, ink text | `Button color=\"accent\"` |\nDefault theme is now `light`. The site follows the viewer's colour scheme; video picks one with `meta.theme`.\n",
 "channel-physics": "# Channel brand (physics): extra layouts, simulations and formulas\n\nUse this when `brandTheme` is `\"channel\"`. The channel brand has every normal layout (title, bullets, stat, steps, outro...) **plus** the physics layouts below. The `handson` brand does not have them, and `check_scenes.py` / `validate_scenes` reject them there.\n\nSet it in the scenes file: `\"meta\": { \"brandTheme\": \"channel\" }`. Default light or dark comes from the brand (dark for the channel). `meta.brand` still overrides the name and mark in the top bar.\n\n## Who is watching: `meta.audience`\n- `\"everyone\"` (the default): the audience is normal people, not physics students. Pictures use plain words (\"Gravity\", \"Speed\", \"One swing takes 2.2 s\"), no vectors or symbols, bigger text, fewer numbers on screen.\n- `\"expert\"`: symbols, vector arrows, KE and PE, formulas in the readouts.\nSet it in `meta` or with `--audience everyone|expert` (MCP option `audience`). Choose `expert` only if the user says the viewers study physics.\n\nWriting for everyone:\n1. Say it in words first. Do not put `equation` on a `sim` scene. Use an `equation` or `derivation` scene only when the narrator says the formula out loud, and keep it to one short line.\n2. Force labels in `freebody` are words: `\"\\\\text{Gravity}\"`, `\"\\\\text{Your push}\"`, `\"\\\\text{Friction}\"`, not `\\\\vec{F}`.\n3. Graph labels in words with units: `\"Time (seconds)\"`, `\"Height (metres)\"`.\n4. Start from something people know (a thrown ball, a swing, a bouncing mattress, a bulb), then show the picture, then give one number.\n5. One idea per scene. If the narrator talks for more than 8 seconds, split it.\n\n## Formulas (`tex`)\nA small LaTeX subset, written inside JSON, so every backslash is doubled: `\"tex\": \"E = \\\\frac{1}{2} m v^2\"`.\n- Fractions `\\\\frac{a}{b}`, roots `\\\\sqrt{x}`, powers `x^2`, `x^{n+1}`, subscripts `v_0`, `F_{\\\\text{net}}`\n- Arrows over letters: `\\\\vec{F}`, `\\\\hat{n}`, `\\\\bar{x}`\n- Greek: `\\\\theta \\\\omega \\\\lambda \\\\mu \\\\Delta \\\\Omega` and the rest of the usual letters\n- Signs: `\\\\cdot \\\\times \\\\pm \\\\approx \\\\propto \\\\neq \\\\leq \\\\geq \\\\rightarrow \\\\infty \\\\partial \\\\nabla \\\\degree`\n- Functions: `\\\\sin \\\\cos \\\\tan \\\\ln \\\\log \\\\exp`\n- Words and units in upright type: `\\\\text{m/s}`, `\\\\text{kg m}`. A thin space is `\\\\,`\n- Highlight a part (a marker sweeps over it, in reading order): `F = \\\\hl{m} \\\\hl{a}`\nKeep one formula to about 40 characters of tex. Long formulas: use `derivation` and show one step per line.\n\n## Layouts\n| layout | needs | optional |\n|---|---|---|\n| `equation` | `tex` | `eyebrow`, `title`, `note`, `size` (`m`, `l`, `xl`; use `m` in 9:16) |\n| `derivation` | `lines` (2 to 5): each a tex string or `{tex, note}` | `title`. The last line is shown in colour as the result |\n| `graph` | `series` (1 to 4): each `{points:[[x,y],...]}` or `{expr:\"0.5*9.8*x^2\"}` | `xLabel`, `yLabel`, `xRange`, `yRange`, `title`, `caption`; per series `label`, `color`, `dashed`, `fill` (shades the area under the curve). With `expr` always set `xRange` |\n| `freebody` | `forces` (1 to 6): `{label (tex), angle (degrees, 0 = right, 90 = up), mag (number), color?}` | `body` (`block`, `ball`), `net: true` (adds the resultant arrow), `title`, `caption` |\n| `quantity` | `name`, `value`, `unit` (tex) | `symbol` (tex), `note`, `eyebrow` |\n| `units` | `rows` (1 to 6): `{name, symbol (tex), unit (tex)}` | `title` |\n| `question` | `question` | `answer` (appears halfway through the scene), `eyebrow` |\n| `analogy` | `left`, `right`: `{title, text?, icon? (Lucide), anim?}` | `title`, `sign` (default `≈`) |\n| `timeline` | `events` (1 to 4): `{year, text}` | `title` |\n| `sim` | `sim` (a name below) | `params`, `show`, `equation` (tex shown beside the title), `title`, `eyebrow`, `caption` |\n\nGraph `expr` accepts `+ - * / ^ ( )`, `2x`, `pi`, `e`, and `sin cos tan asin acos atan exp ln log sqrt abs`. The variable is `x`.\n\n## Simulations (`layout: \"sim\"`)\nEvery simulation is a formula of time, so it looks the same in every render. Pass numbers in `params` (all optional, defaults shown). `speed` makes time run faster or slower where it exists.\n- `projectile`: `v0` 20 (m/s), `angle` 45 (deg), `g` 9.81, `h0` 0 (launch height). The flight is stretched to fill about 80 percent of the scene. `show`: `components` (vx, vy arrows), `gravity`, `range`, `-trail`, `-velocity`\n- `pendulum`: `length` 1 (m), `theta0` 40 (deg), `damping` 0, `speed` 1. Real swing (not the small-angle formula) and kinetic/potential energy bars. `show`: `force`, `-energy`\n- `spring`: `m` 1, `k` 4, `c` 0.25 (damping), `x0` 1, `speed`. Shows an x-against-t trace and energy bars. `show`: `-graph`, `-energy`, `-force`\n- `incline`: `angle` 30, `mu` 0.2 (friction), `m` 1. If friction is too big the block stays still (it is shown, as it is true). `show`: `-forces`\n- `collision`: `m1` 2, `m2` 1, `v1` 3, `v2` -1, `e` 1 (1 elastic, 0 sticks together), `x1` -5, `x2` 4. The scene is timed so the hit is at 40 percent. Shows momentum and kinetic energy. `show`: `-energy`\n- `orbit`: `speed` 1 (1 is a circle, below 1 an ellipse, 1.41 or more escapes), `rate` 1. `show`: `-gravity`, `-velocity`\n- `wave`: `mode` (`travel`, `standing`, `superposition`), `A` 1, `wavelength` 4, `freq` 0.5. `show`: `wavelength`, `-particle`\n- `optics`: `n1` 1, `n2` 1.5, `angle` 40, `from` (start angle; the ray sweeps from `from` to `angle`). Total internal reflection appears when it happens (try `n1` 1.5, `n2` 1, `angle` 60)\n- `circuit`: `V` 12, `R1` 4, `R2` 2, `mode` (`series`, `parallel`). Electrons move faster where the current is bigger\n- `field`: `q1` 1, `q2` -1 (sign sets the colour), `d` 2.4 (half the distance). An arrow grid\n- `gas`: `n` 60, `T` 1, `T2` (end temperature, the gas heats up smoothly), `seed` 7, `speed` 1.6\n\n## Colours that always mean the same thing\nForce red, velocity blue, acceleration orange, energy yellow, field purple, positive charge red, negative charge blue, trace and path teal. Use these names for `color`: `force velocity accel energy field positive negative mass axis grid trace`, or a pastel name, or a hex code.\n\n## How to write a physics video\n1. Question first (`question`), then the idea (`equation` or `analogy`), then **show it moving** (`sim` or `graph`), then the numbers (`quantity`, `units`), then the close (`outro`).\n2. One idea per scene. Put the formula on the screen only while it is spoken. Use `derivation` when the narration walks through steps.\n3. In 9:16 keep formulas short, use `size: \"m\"`, and prefer `sim` and `graph` (they stack vertically).\n4. The picture must match the numbers in the narration: if the narrator says 45 degrees, `angle` is 45.\n\n## Adding another brand (for the developer)\n1. Copy `src/brands/channel/` to `src/brands/<name>/`, edit its `theme.ts` (colours, fonts, name) and its scenes.\n2. Add it in `src/brands/themes.ts` (`THEMES`) and `src/brands/scenes.ts` (`REGISTRIES`).\n3. Add its layouts and brand name to `assets/channel-schema.json`, and the name to `--brand-theme` choices in `scripts/build_video.py`.\n4. Run `python3 mcp/build_guide.py`, then deploy the MCP.\n"
};
